    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d345">

                <pb facs="0509" n="397" xml:id="pg397"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.01 Outer Mongolia/33: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in the Soviet Union</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Bullitt</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Extract]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Moscow</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-11-02T18:00:00+03:00">November 2, 1935—6
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received November 2—3:15 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>472. In the course of a brief conversation today Litvinov said that he was
                    absolutely certain that the Japanese were not contemplating any immediate
                    advance against Outer Mongolia. He asserted that he believed the Japanese
                    intended now to make every effort to dominate North and Central China. He said
                    that he believed China would be compelled to fight in the near future or be
                    extinguished as an independent nation.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bullitt</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
